INT. DOUBLE-DECKER TOUR BUS, EMPTY, PARKED IN DEPOT - DAY

A red open-top bus, parked indoors under fluorescent lights. Plastic seats. A microphone on a coiled cord. Nobody aboard but two men.

GUS BELLOWES-ADEYEMI (50s, tour-guide blazer, sweaty) stands at the front with the microphone. Seated three rows back, arms crossed, is FERDINAND "FERDY" LAGRANGE (40s, same blazer, one size smaller), clutching a clipboard.

GUS
(into microphone, cheerful)
Ladies and gentlemen, welcome aboard the Grand Heritage Tour!

He waves at the empty seats. Ferdy scribbles on the clipboard.

FERDY
You're not smiling with your eyes.

GUS
I'm smiling with my mouth.

FERDY
That's the problem.

Gus lowers the mic. Raises it. Clears his throat.

GUS
(into microphone)
To your left, the Old Customs House, built in eighteen...

He glances at his index card. Turns it over. Turns it back.

GUS (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Eighteen what.

FERDY
Eighteen-forty.

GUS
Eighteen-forty. To your left, built in eighteen-forty--

FERDY
Forty-one.

GUS
It says forty here.

FERDY
It was wrong. I fixed it.

Gus slowly lowers the microphone to his chest.

GUS
You rewrote my card.

FERDY
I corrected your card.

GUS
It's my route.

FERDY
It's my company.

GUS
Your uncle's company.

FERDY
He left it to me.

GUS
He left it to you because you were standing there.

Ferdy stands. The bus rocks. Gus takes a step toward the aisle. The microphone cord pulls taut between them like a leash.

FERDY
(to the empty seats)
Ladies and gentlemen, please excuse the driver, who has a problem with authority.

GUS
(to the empty seats)
Ladies and gentlemen, please disregard the owner, who has never driven this bus once.

FERDY
I have too!

GUS
You hit the carousel.

FERDY
That carousel was in the road.

GUS
It's been there since the nineteen-twenties!

Ferdy lunges for the microphone. Gus holds it above his head. Ferdy jumps, trips on the cord, and lands on the seat with the mic in his lap. Gus stands over him, panting. Neither of them says anything. Gus is absolutely furious. Ferdy looks up at him, wounded and stunned.

FERDY
(small)
You're right. I hit the carousel.

Gus's face falls from fury to bewilderment.

GUS
...That's it?

FERDY
That's it.

GUS
You're just going to say that?

FERDY
I've wanted to say it for six years.

Gus sits down heavily on the seat across the aisle. He rubs his face with both hands.

GUS
He was in the middle of the road, Ferdy.

FERDY
The carousel?

GUS
The pony. The one in the middle.

FERDY
Was that why you took the left?

GUS
I thought it was a real pony.

They stare at each other. Ferdy's lip twitches. Gus's does too. Then Ferdy snorts, and Gus wheezes, and the two men fall into helpless, honking laughter, rocking the whole bus.

FERDY
(gasping)
A real pony.

GUS
(wiping his eyes)
Don't.

FERDY
In the middle of the road.

GUS
Stop.

Their laughter dies slowly. Ferdy lifts the microphone, holds it between them like a peace pipe, and offers it to Gus.

FERDY
(into microphone, to the empty seats)
Ladies and gentlemen. This is Gus. He knows the way.

He passes it over. Gus looks at it for a long moment, then stands, steps to the front, and clears his throat.

GUS
(into microphone, gently)
To your left, the Old Customs House. Built in eighteen-forty-one.

FERDY
Thank you.

GUS
(muttering)
Eighteen-forty.
